🚽 Test case: #196 -- a brace-initialized array of struct/union POINTERS
🚽 (`PointPtr values[2] = {&r};`) is checked element-by-element against
🚽 the array's declared type/tag, the same as a plain scalar initializer
🚽 (PR #248 review, round 4, finding 1). This is a FOURTH place a
🚽 pointer-to-struct value gets stored (distinct from struct_init_expr,
🚽 data.op.right, and plain assignment, all covered by earlier rounds):
🚽 create_multi_array_declaration_node() leaves data.op.right NULL and
🚽 puts the initializer on pending_initializer instead, which
🚽 semantic_check_expression_list() only ever VISITED, never compared
🚽 against the array's own declared type/tag.
gang Point {
    rizz x;
    rizz y;
};
gang Rect {
    rizz w;
    rizz h;
};

lit gang Point *PointPtr;

skibidi main {
    gang Rect r;
    r.w = 11;
    r.h = 22;
    PointPtr values[2] = { &r };
    yapping("%d %d", values[0].x, values[0].y);
}
